PrismRTG - RTG for AmigaOS 3.x
=======================

A from-scratch retargetable-graphics system for two boards:
  * Village Tronic Picasso II / GBAPII++ (Cirrus GD542x), Zorro II
  * Piccolo, Piccolo SD64, GVP Spectrum 28/24, Ateo Graffity, Picasso IV -
    emulator-tested only
  * MNT ZZ9000, Zorro III
  * Z3660 accelerator's RTG output (A4000)

It takes the place of Picasso96: the two cannot both be active on one
Amiga, and PrismRTG does not start while Picasso96's monitor files are in
DEVS:Monitors. Needs Kickstart 3.1+ and a 68020 or better (no FPU needed).
The driver takes about 335 KB of fast RAM (none of chip RAM); a Workbench
at 800x600 16-bit with MUI and a network stack runs in 2 MB chip + 2 MB
fast, and 4 MB fast leaves room for the usual programs.

Install:   double-click Install_PrismRTG (the standard Installer), or from a
           Shell in this drawer:  Installer Install_PrismRTG MINUSER AVERAGE
           It finds the card, sets the Workbench screen and, if Picasso96
           is installed, lists its monitor files and asks: back them up
           (moved to SYS:Storage/Picasso96-parked, never deleted) or leave
           them (then PrismRTG stays off). Remove moves them back. With
           Picasso96's settings on the Amiga it offers to switch on the
           same screen sizes (C:PrismImport; Picasso96's file is only read).
Update, install afresh (new settings) or remove: run Install_PrismRTG again.

What goes where
  C:PrismD               the driver; started at boot
  LIBS:Prism/#?.driver   one module per kind of board; PrismD loads the
                         one it needs. A driver for another board is one
                         more file here (docs/writing-a-driver.md on
                         GitHub); BOARD=<name> selects it, Auto tries it
  C:PrismSetup           the installer's helper (card detection)
  C:PrismProbe           lists the expansion boards PrismRTG can drive;
                         REPORT makes a report for a card it doesn't find
  C:PrismImport          Picasso96's screen sizes into PrismRTG's settings
  DEVS:Monitors/Prism    starts PrismD before Workbench opens
  SYS:Prefs/PrismPrefs   modes, refresh rates, board, blitter, palette, log
  ENVARC:Prism.prefs     the settings

Using it
  Reboot, open Prefs/ScreenMode and choose a PRISM: mode (8bit or 16bit).
  Programs that ask for CyberGraphX or for the Picasso96 API get PrismRTG's
  own built-in cybergraphics.library and Picasso96API.library.

New in 1.3 beta 6
  When PrismRTG doesn't start at boot, it says why: PrismPrefs shows the
  reason on its bottom line ("Not running: no supported board"), and
  PrismProbe's report has everything PrismD said (T:PrismD.log).
  A start that failed by itself - no board, a setting it can't use - no
  longer makes the next boot skip PrismRTG. Only a start that hangs does,
  as before; fixing a setting and rebooting now just works.
  A card driven through its Picasso96 driver and still held by a start
  that failed: PrismD says "reboot" plainly.

New in 1.3 beta 5
  A card PrismRTG doesn't find: double-click PrismProbe in this drawer
  (or run PrismProbe REPORT). It only reads, and saves a report to
  RAM:PrismRTG-report.txt - the system, every expansion board and the
  driver that claimed it, PrismRTG's state and settings, the other
  graphics software, and when it was made - for a "card report" on
  GitHub. The drawing code is the same as in beta 4.
  The installer gives this package drawer the Workbench's own drawer
  icon once it has run.

New in 1.3 beta 4 (please report how it goes)
  Span: the second monitor keeps its half of Workbench while another
  screen is in front on the first one (a test picture, a game's own
  screen, a chipset screen). It went black until the span screen came
  back to the front.
  Refresh rates: with a card driven through its Picasso96 driver,
  PrismD's mode list no longer shows rates the card never gave, such as
  "1920x1080 8 Hz" on an Apollo A6000 (issue #25). A mode without real
  timing has no rate listed; the card times it as before.

New in 1.3 beta 3 (please report how it goes)
  Text in fonts with kerning (most proportional bitmap fonts, such as the
  ones Amelinium uses) is as wide again as graphics.library draws it: each
  character moved on by its spacing but not its kerning, so a line came out
  narrower than TextLength() said. Programs that place their cursor or
  clear behind the text by that width left old pixels standing; in
  Amelinium's address bar the cursor grew and edited addresses showed
  leftover characters (issue #25).

New in 1.3 beta 2 (please report how it goes)
  Span: one screen across both monitors. "Span right" or "Span left" in
  PrismPrefs' 2nd monitor setting gives every 8- and 16-bit mode both
  boards have a span mode twice as wide; Workbench on one of those reaches
  over both monitors, and the pointer and windows move across the middle.
  The primary board shows its half itself; the second board's half is a
  copy, converted where the two boards store pixels differently (a ZZ9000
  and a GBAPII++ two pixels at a time). PrismSetup WBID=$0182 (for
  example) sets a span mode for Workbench from a script.

New in 1.3 beta 1 (please report how it goes)
  Two boards, two monitors: the second monitor is never black any more.
  PrismPrefs' new "2nd monitor" setting (SECONDMONITOR=) picks what it
  shows while it has no screen of its own: "Own screen" (the default) opens
  a public screen, PRISM2, on it for windows and programs; "Mirror" shows a
  copy of the first monitor; "Off" leaves the Amiga's own video there, as
  before. Push the pointer off the edge facing the other monitor and it
  moves over, bringing that board's front screen forward (SECONDSIDE=LEFT
  when the second monitor stands on the left).
  Smooth screen dragging (issue #1): the Cirrus cards (GBAPII++, Picasso
  II and IV) and the ZZ9000 now show a dragged screen and the one behind
  it with the card's own split display, so a drag is a few register writes
  per step instead of a copy of both screens; the card's pointer stays on,
  and PrismD runs at 60 steps a second. On an A4000 the drag went from
  about 5 steps a second to smooth on both cards. Dragging is still
  switched on with "Drag screens" in PrismPrefs (DRAGGING=ON).
  Everything in 1.2.3 is in here too.

New in 1.2.3
  Workbench icons without a selected image of their own (ShowConfig and
  PrepCard in Tools, simple two-colour icons) came out as an empty box or
  in the wrong colours when selected on a 256-colour PrismRTG screen. The
  "invert through mask" blit now inverts, and a blit from a planar image
  changes only as many planes as the image has, as on the Amiga's own
  chips. Found and fixed by Ferenc Boldog (fboldog).

New in 1.2.2
  ZZ9000 (firmware 2.8 and newer): a video layer the card shows itself (a
  player's YUV frames in the card's memory on a 16- or 32-bit screen) came
  out as a black window on a real card: the firmware takes the layer but
  shows it only once its overlay feature is switched on, which PrismRTG
  never did. It does now, the way ZZ9000.card does; the emulator used for
  testing didn't have that switch, and has it now.
  Workbench 3.2 froze the machine when a dragged icon was dropped where it
  can't go (the screen's title bar). Workbench drags icons as Bobs, and on
  a refused drop it calls RemIBob, which PrismRTG left to the ROM: the
  ROM's version walks lists that only its own drawing keeps, and on a
  PrismRTG screen it walked them for ever. RemIBob is now handled like
  DrawGList (the Bob is taken off and the backgrounds put back).

New in 1.2.1
  FS-UAE showed no screen larger than 640x480: its older UAE core answers
  the question "how large a screen can the card show?" with the core's
  built-in default (640x480), where WinUAE and Amiberry answer with the
  largest size the host display can do. PrismRTG no longer takes that
  default for a limit; the card's memory decides. FS-UAE 3.1.66 now offers
  every size (33 modes instead of 12).

New in 1.2 (everything since 1.1.1, in short; the betas follow below)
  Faster: icons and other masked images 2-9x (only the pixels the mask
  lets through are drawn, images in the board's memory copied by the
  board); Workbench drawers through PeterK's icon.library copied plane to
  plane; window dragging as quick as under Picasso96, with the blits that
  swap a moving window and its hidden copy done by the card - through a
  card's Picasso96 driver, on UAE's card, on the Picasso II family and on
  the ZZ9000 in Zorro III mode, now at every depth.
  Fixed: at 24 and 32 bits those blits (and every BltBitMap minterm that is
  not a plain copy) worked on palette pens instead of the pixels' bits, so
  a dragged window could come back with fewer colours, and each blit took
  seconds; they are bitwise now, like the Amiga blitter and Picasso96.
  More sizes through a card's own Picasso96 driver (a Vampire, a
  PiStorm's VideoCore); Caffeine OS set up by the installer; the OS 3.9
  Installer works; ClipBlit to and from off-screen planar buffers right at
  every depth.
  Thanks to HANSolo-0007 for the Vampire reports behind several of these.

New in 1.2 beta 6
  The quicker window dragging of beta 5 on PrismRTG's own drivers too: on
  the Picasso II family (Picasso II and II+, GBAPII++, Piccolo, Piccolo
  SD64, Spectrum, Picasso IV) and on the ZZ9000 in Zorro III mode, the
  blits that swap a moving window with its hidden copy run on the card at
  8 and 16 bit. On an A2000's GBAPII++ such a blit took 217 ms, now less
  than a timer tick; on an A4000's ZZ9000 108 ms, now 1 ms. The ZZ9000
  gets only the blit types its firmware computes correctly (testing on a
  real card found two that come out wrong); the others stay on the CPU.

New in 1.2 beta 5
  Dragging windows on cards driven through their Picasso96 driver
  (BOARD=P96) and on UAE's card is as quick as under Picasso96 again: the
  blits that swap a moving window with its hidden copy (XOR) now go to the
  card instead of the CPU. A 700x450 browser window took 1.9 seconds per
  drag step in WinUAE, now 0.13. Natively driven boards are unchanged.
  Under WinUAE, Prism through UAE's Picasso96 driver (P96CARD=uaegfx.card)
  no longer shows a garbled picture.

New in 1.2 beta 4
  More screen sizes on cards driven through their own Picasso96 driver
  (BOARD=P96: a Vampire, a PiStorm's VideoCore): 960x540, which was in the
  list but could never be shown there, and the larger sizes 1280x800,
  1366x768, 1600x900, 1680x1050, 1600x1200, 1920x1200 and 2560x1440 (switch
  those on in PrismPrefs). A card that can't show one leaves it out, as
  before.

New in 1.2 beta 3
  A fix for the update check (PrismUpdate, and Check in PrismPrefs): with
  AmiSSL 5 it could crash the Amiga (gurus of every kind, mostly on the
  second or third check). Started from a Shell with a small stack it ran a
  second copy of itself meant to have 64 KB of stack, but that copy got the
  Shell's 4 KB, and AmiSSL overran it. It now runs on its own 64 KB stack.
  Install this one by hand: beta 2's check may crash while looking for it.
  The same fix is in 1.1.1.
  PrismBench checks ClipBlit from a planar buffer in fast RAM into one in
  chip RAM as well.

New in 1.2 beta 2
  Install this one by hand: the update check in 1.1 and 1.2 beta 1 can't
  find it. GitHub began sending its release information pretty-printed,
  and PrismUpdate found nothing in it ("could not check"); it reads both
  forms now, and a beta also sees the newest beta even while a full
  release is GitHub's Latest.
  Workbench drawers open faster: Workbench (with PeterK's icon.library)
  draws through planar bitmaps in fast RAM, which the blitter can't
  reach; PrismRTG now copies those plane to plane instead of through
  chunky pixels and back. The Prefs drawer on an Amiga 2000 (68030,
  GBAPII++, 16-bit Workbench) opens in 2.1 s instead of 3.4 s.
  Fixed: ClipBlit between a window and an off-screen planar RastPort
  (a program saving what is under something) - it was black at 16 bits
  and more, and is now done by PrismRTG in both directions; a 16/24/32-
  bit screen copied into a planar bitmap came out black.
  Caffeine OS (tested in WinUAE): the installer finds UAE's RTG card and
  cards that have a Picasso96 driver (a PiStorm's VideoCore, a Vampire)
  and sets PrismRTG up to drive them through it; BOARD=AUTO falls back to
  UAE's card and to that driver when none of PrismRTG's own boards is
  there. A Startup-Sequence whose LoadMonDrvs loads only the monitors it
  names gets a few lines that start PrismRTG (asked first, the old file
  kept as S:Startup-Sequence.before-prism), and the Workbench screen mode
  is also set in its per-machine copies (screenmode.prefs.UAE and .PI).
  Removing PrismRTG undoes all of it. The installer also works with the
  OS 3.9 Installer now (it refused the whole script).

New in 1.2 beta 1
  Icons and other masked images (BltMaskBitMapRastPort, how Workbench
  draws icons) are drawn much faster: only the pixels the mask lets
  through are written, the screen is no longer read back for them, and an
  image already in the board's memory is copied by the board. Against 1.1,
  on an Amiga 2000 (68030, GBAPII++) 2.2-4x at 8 bits and up to 7x at
  24/32 bits; on an Amiga 4000 (68060, ZZ9000) 2-3.4x, 9x at 32 bits.
  PrismBench measures them and checks every pixel. Tested on both.

New in 1.1 (everything since 1.0.2, in short; the betas follow below)
  More boards: PrismRTG drives a card through its existing Picasso96 card
  driver (BOARD=P96), UAE's own RTG card, the Z3660's RTG output (by
  Jusii), and - in emulators so far - Piccolo, Piccolo SD64, Spectrum
  28/24, Graffity and Picasso IV. Board drivers are modules in LIBS:Prism
  with a frozen interface (Stefan Reinauer's work). Two boards at once,
  each with its own monitor. Screen mode promotion, your own sizes, video
  layers for players, gamma, oversized screens with autoscroll, big
  pointers, a software pointer, monitor power saving, chipset screens on
  the board, an ARexx port, Picasso96's sizes imported, and an update
  check. Faster drawing throughout. Needs Kickstart 3.1 or newer.
  Since beta 9: PrismPrefs opens on its own screen when the Workbench is
  too short for its window; the update check says which step failed (name
  lookup, connection, TLS, or GitHub's hourly limit); a new drawer icon;
  PrismPrefs started from its icon no longer leaves an empty window open.
  Thanks to Stefan Reinauer, Jusii, Dimitris Panokostas (midwan), polluks,
  yelworC, halle-saale and HANSolo-0007 - see Credits on GitHub.

New in 1.1 beta 9
  Speed: text in 8-pixel-wide fonts, ScrollRaster and ClipBlit within a
  window are faster (on a 68030 with a Picasso II+: 40-character text 30%,
  ScrollRaster 13%, ClipBlit 6% over beta 8), and the ZZ9000 driver hands
  each operation to the board in fewer writes. Log: SYNC no longer holds up
  the display, and a log switched on while PrismD runs now logs.
New in 1.1 beta 8
  Monitor power saving: "Sleep" in PrismPrefs, the minutes without input
  before the monitors go dark (off by default). The Picasso II family and
  the ZZ9000 (firmware 2.7+) put the monitor to sleep; other boards show
  black. Chipset screens on the board: "Mirror chipset" copies a chipset
  screen that promotion leaves alone (its own bitmap, written straight into
  the bitplanes) to the board, a lores one at twice its size - for
  card-only monitors. An ARexx port, PRISM. C:PrismImport takes the screen
  sizes switched on in Picasso96, and the installer offers it on a first
  install. cybergraphics.library 43: WritePixelArrayAlpha, BltTemplateAlpha
  and ProcessPixelArray. The Picasso IV (Zorro II and III) on PrismRTG's own
  driver, emulator-tested only. Long vertical lines (window frames) faster
  on boards without hardware lines. The driver interface is frozen: a
  driver built for this release keeps working in later ones (drivers built
  for earlier betas must be rebuilt once).
New in 1.1 beta 7
  The Z3660's RTG output, by Jusii: a driver for the Z3660 accelerator's
  HDMI framebuffer (firmware 1.03 or newer, autoconfig_rtg YES), with its
  hardware pointer, the monitor switch (monitor_switch in z3660cfg.txt, or
  ENV:Z3660-MONSWITCH), and fills, copies, text, lines and planar blits on
  its blitter. Written and tested on his Z3660.
  Seven more sizes in PrismPrefs (1280x800, 1600x1200, 1920x1200,
  1680x1050, 1600x900, 1366x768, 2560x1440), off until you switch them on,
  shown where the board can (Jusii). Workbench stays on the card after a
  ScreenMode change that keeps a smaller size than the mode (Jusii). A
  Workbench size larger than its PRISM mode is not opened on the card: OS
  3.2 cuts it to the mode's size, OS 3.1 falls back to a chipset Workbench -
  set the size to the mode's own there.

New in 1.1 beta 6 (tested in emulators only - please report what you see)
  Two graphics boards at once, each with its own monitor (PRISM: and
  PRISM2:) and front screen. Screen mode promotion: programs that open a
  chipset screen get it on the board (PrismPrefs "Promote screens", with a
  per-program list). Four sizes of your own in the mode list, with VESA
  timings computed on the Picasso II family. Video layers for players
  (cgxvideo.library and the Picasso96 PIP with YUV formats); on a ZZ9000
  with the BlitterStudio firmware 2.8+ the board shows them itself. Gamma
  for 256-colour screens. Oversized screens with autoscroll. Pointers
  bigger than the board's sprite. Update check over HTTPS in PrismPrefs
  (needs AmiSSL 5). The installer updates, installs afresh or removes.
  PrismPrefs and PrismSetup can be translated (no translations yet; see
  docs/translating.md on GitHub). This ReadMe also comes as PrismRTG.guide.
  Faster on a 68030 than beta 5: fills +11-12%, ReadPixel +95%, lines +23%.
  Fixed: the pointer stopping short on screens wider than 1280 (#1),
  dragging, the software pointer under drawing, flicker of a video layer
  in a blitted band, and many smaller things from two code reviews.

New in 1.1 beta 5
  From the first reports on hardware I don't have - an Apollo Vampire
  through its P96 driver, and a MiSTer's ZZ9000 core. Thank you.
  Dragged Workbench icons are shown again (they were drawn where nothing
  could see them). On the P96 driver board, copies from an off-screen
  bitmap to a window use the card's blitter: browsers scroll three to six
  times faster in WinUAE; the same on UAE (native). A card whose own
  planar blit or text blit gets a test picture wrong is drawn on the CPU
  instead (the Vampire's planar blit at 16 bits; the MiSTer's text) - the
  log says so. The pointer no longer lags when the disk is busy.
  New sizes 960x540 and 1920x1080 where the board can show them.
  PrismPrefs has Drag screens and Soft pointer switches. While dragging, a
  screen of another size behind the dragged one stays black (the card
  shows one mode at a time). Small fills, copies and text 1-3% faster on a
  68030. PrismCheck reports how the board is driven.

New in 1.1 beta 4 "Tequila" - please update from beta 3
  Fixes a crash beta 3 could hit: drawing text used almost 2 KB of the
  calling program's stack (Workbench, IPrefs - often 4 KB in all).
  Thirteen bugs from a code review fixed: among them ScrollRaster in a
  partly covered window, a pointer that could be copied with the screen,
  a stale palette cache, and quitting PrismD while programs still draw.
  Faster: pixels, lines and small blits 3-11%; text on the native UAE
  driver five times; and dragging a screen no longer eats the machine -
  the compositor redoes only what changed and copies the dragged screen
  on the card's blitter. 35 KB less fast RAM.
  New board: Ateo Concepts Graffity (Zorro II and III), emulation only.
  A driver for a new board is one file: src/drv_template.c, built with
  tools/mkdriver.sh, loaded by name or found by itself in LIBS:Prism.
  docs/writing-a-driver.md and docs/driver-development.md on GitHub.
  Tested on every board WinUAE emulates: docs/emulated-cards.md.

New in 1.1 beta 3
  Needs Kickstart 3.1 or newer (3.0 never worked; PrismD now says so).
  The board drivers are separate modules in LIBS:Prism, drawing goes
  through format-aware operations a driver can decline or fail safely,
  and Picasso96 card drivers can bring their own memory handling - all
  Stefan Reinauer's work, with speed and robustness changes on top.
  Software pointer for boards without a working hardware pointer.

New in 1.1 beta (experimental - please report how it goes)
  Two more boards, chosen by hand: Board in PrismPrefs, or BOARD= in
  ENVARC:Prism.prefs. Neither is picked automatically.
  "UAE (native)"  BOARD=UAEGFX - UAE's own RTG card in WinUAE/Amiberry,
                  without Picasso96.
  "P96 driver"    BOARD=P96 P96CARD=<file> - drives a card through its
                  existing Picasso96 card driver (.card), without the rest
                  of Picasso96. Tested with UAE's uaegfx.card; reported
                  working on an Apollo Vampire with vampiregfx.card.
  Details: docs/uaegfx.md and docs/p96-drivers.md on GitHub.

Two boards (new in 1.1 beta 6 - tested in an emulator so far)
  With two boards in one machine - a ZZ9000 and a GBAPII++ or Picasso II -
  each on its own monitor, PrismRTG drives both. The first board's modes
  are PRISM:..., the second's PRISM2:...; pick a board by picking a mode
  (ScreenMode, a program's mode requester). Each board shows its own front
  screen, and the pointer is on the board of the screen in front.
  Any two of the supported cards work, two of the same kind too. Either one
  can be the primary (PRISM: modes, so Workbench): Board "Auto, swapped" in
  PrismPrefs (PRIMARY=SECOND) makes the second board found the primary - say,
  the card on the bigger monitor; the status line names the boards and their
  expansion addresses. "Auto, 1 board" (SECONDBOARD=OFF) keeps to one board;
  with two, the Test button asks which board shows the test picture.
  "2nd monitor" (SECONDMONITOR=) is what the second monitor shows while no
  screen of its own is on it: Own screen (PRISM2, a public screen for
  windows and programs: PUBSCREEN=PRISM2, or a PRISM2: mode), Mirror (a
  copy of the first board's picture, in its mode of the same size and
  depth; the pointer is not copied yet) or Off (the Amiga's own video).
  The pointer pushed past the edge facing the other monitor (no button
  held) goes over to it; SECONDSIDE=LEFT if the second monitor stands left
  of the first.
  Span right / Span left (SECONDMONITOR=SPAN, from 1.3 beta 2): one
  screen across both monitors. Each 8- or 16-bit mode both boards have
  gets a span mode twice as wide on the primary ("PRISM:1600x600 16bit
  span" from two 800x600 modes); pick it in ScreenMode for Workbench, or in
  a program's mode requester. The primary shows its half, the second board
  a copy of the other half, and the pointer and windows move freely across
  the middle. "Span right" when the second monitor stands right of the
  first, "Span left" when it stands on the left. The copied half follows
  what is drawn a moment later - on a Zorro II board a whole half takes
  about a third of a second.

Software pointer
  Boards without a working hardware pointer get a pointer drawn into the
  screen; Soft pointer in PrismPrefs (SOFTWAREPOINTER=ON in the prefs
  file) forces it, e.g. on a MiSTer.
  It costs a few percent of drawing speed.

Screen mode promotion
  "Promote screens" in PrismPrefs (PROMOTE=ON) opens a screen a program
  asks for on PAL, NTSC or another chipset monitor on the board instead,
  in the smallest 256-colour mode that holds it, at the depth asked for.
  The Programs list makes exceptions: a program kept on the chipset with
  the switch on, or promoted with it off (KEEPPROG= / PROMOTEPROG=, by
  command name or task name). The screen is the mode's size, so a 640x256
  screen drawn edge to edge leaves a band; programs with their own bitmap
  or more than 8 planes are left alone. PrismD reads this when it starts.

Video layers (overlay)
  PrismD carries its own cgxvideo.library and the Picasso96 PIP calls
  take the YUV formats, so video players (FroggerNG with DISPLAY=VLAYER
  or DISPLAY=P96, and others that use either) show their picture in a
  window. The card has no overlay engine, so the frames are converted
  and scaled by the CPU: small windows on a 68030, full ones on an 060.

Large pointers
  A pointer bigger than the board's sprite (the ZZ9000's is 32x48) is
  drawn by the software pointer, whole, instead of being cut.

Oversized screens
  A screen opened with autoscroll and a size beyond its mode (ScreenMode's
  "Autoscroll" with a bigger size, or a program's own) is scrolled by
  Intuition when the pointer pushes an edge, on the Picasso II family and
  the ZZ9000.

Gamma
  "Gamma" in PrismPrefs (GAMMA=1.4 in the prefs file): the palette's
  curve for 256-colour screens and the Test picture. Deeper screens are
  not touched (their colours are in the pixels, not the DAC).

Your own sizes
  The mode list ends with four "custom" entries: pick one, type a width
  (a multiple of 8) and a height, Set size, and Test shows it at once.
  PrismD offers it after a restart at every depth the board manages.
  The Picasso II family computes a VESA timing for any size within its
  pixel clock (1152x864, 1360x768 ...); the ZZ9000 shows the sizes its
  firmware has (1600x1200, 720x576, 2560x1440 ...). In the prefs file:
  CUSTOM1=1152x864 .. CUSTOM4=.

Update check
  "Updates" in PrismPrefs (GitHub, a mirror, or GitHub then the mirror)
  and "Check now": C:PrismUpdate asks for the newest release over HTTPS
  and offers to download and install it (the installer then runs as
  usual, and the update takes effect at the next reboot). Needs a
  running TCP/IP stack and AmiSSL 5, or an SSL offload with the AmiSSL
  API. A mirror is MIRROR=https://host/dir/ in the prefs file, a
  directory with latest.txt ("version archive"). From a Shell:
  PrismUpdate, PrismUpdate GET.

Monitor power saving
  "Sleep" in PrismPrefs (POWERSAVE=10 in the prefs file): after that many
  minutes without a key or mouse event the board's picture goes dark, and
  the next key or mouse move brings it back. 0, the default, is never.
  The Picasso II family turns its picture and syncs off, so the monitor
  sleeps, as does the ZZ9000 with firmware 2.7 or newer; on the Picasso IV,
  UAE and P96 cards the screen goes black. Use and Save in PrismPrefs take effect at once.

Chipset screens on the board
  "Mirror chipset" in PrismPrefs (MIRRORCHIPSET=ON): a chipset screen in
  front that promotion left alone (its own bitmap, or written straight into
  the bitplanes) is shown on the board as a copy, a lores one at twice its
  size, for card-only monitors. Not HAM or dual playfield. Switching it on
  or off takes effect at the next reboot (unlike Sleep, it is read when
  PrismD starts).

ARexx
  PrismD has an ARexx port, PRISM (RexxMast must run): VERSION, BOARDS,
  BOARD, VRAM, MODES, MODE <n>, MODEID <w> <h> <bits>, TEST <w> <h> <bits>
  [hz] and TESTEND (the Test picture), POWERSAVE [min], SLEEP, WAKE, DARK,
  LOG [ON|OFF], HELP. docs/arexx.md on GitHub has the details.

Picasso96's sizes
  C:PrismImport reads DEVS:Picasso96Settings and switches on every size
  and depth that was on there; sizes PrismRTG lacks become custom sizes
  (up to four). PrismImport TEST shows what it would do; ONLY switches
  every other mode off. PrismD takes the list at its next start.

Dragging and PIP windows
  Vertical dragging between RTG screens (DRAGGING=ON) and P96 memory-window
  PIPs go through a compositor that redoes only what changed and copies the
  dragged screen with the card's blitter; drawing on a dragged screen keeps
  most of its speed on a 68030. Switched off (dragging's default) they
  cost nothing. Few programs have used it yet - please report.
  Where the card can split its display (the Cirrus cards and the ZZ9000;
  from 1.3) a drag of two screens of the same depth needs no copying:
  the card shows the top one down to the drag line and the other below.
  On the Cirrus cards the lower screen must start at the beginning of the
  card's memory, so the first step of a drag may swap two bitmaps there
  (a moment's glimpse of another screen); later steps are instant.

24-bit or 32-bit?
  Both are true colour. On the GBAPII++ the card's blitter can draw text
  and fills at 32-bit but not at 24-bit (text is several times faster),
  while copying and scrolling are about a quarter faster at 24-bit because
  each pixel is smaller. 24-bit is 640x480 only there; 32-bit also does
  800x600. The Picasso II has 24-bit only, the ZZ9000 32-bit only.

If something goes wrong
  Hold the LEFT MOUSE BUTTON while booting: PrismRTG doesn't start and
  Workbench opens on a native mode. Then use PrismPrefs (switch a mode
  off, change its refresh, or turn the blitter off) and reboot.
  With Log "On" in PrismPrefs, PrismD writes T:PrismD.log. "Sync" writes
  each line to SYS:PrismD.log at once - slow, but it is there after a
  machine that stops while starting is reset.
  256-colour screens with red and blue exchanged (a real Piccolo or
  Spectrum may do this): tick "Swap red/blue" in PrismPrefs.

Reporting a problem
  Download PrismCheck from the same release page, run it, and attach
  the report it writes to an issue at
  https://github.com/lainejones/PrismRTG/issues

  A card PrismRTG doesn't find: double-click PrismProbe in this drawer
  (or run PrismProbe REPORT). It only reads, never touches a board, and
  saves a report to RAM:PrismRTG-report.txt. Attach it to an issue
  there: New issue, then "A graphics card PrismRTG doesn't find".

